2.1.0 (unreleased)
----------
* Fix infinite loop when using --blocksize larger than 1GB (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/4)
* Fix output artefacts, and show the status on several lines, aligned and in color (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/8)
* Fix build on Apple Silicon (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/commit/e909d314cc4d575fc3ed7b8cb5fabd870c530d0a)
* Show warnings by default (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/commit/764147e3053e14c85900a16c4104de31d1cc4f7d)
* Require Rust 1.83+ for building from source (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/1)
* Fix a busy loop that used up a CPU core while waiting for random data, which slowed down wipes of devices that are faster than the random generator (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/6)
* Read RDRAND one block ahead in a thread that keeps running, instead of in a new thread for each block, which makes generating random data faster (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/7)
* When wiping a device or partition, show a progress bar and the estimated time until the wipe is finished (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/12)
* Add --deterministic and --verify to check whether a device really stores the data written to it (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/15, https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/17)
* Allow continuing an interrupted deterministic wipe with --deterministic=<seed> and --skip-bytes (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/16)
* Reject --blocksize 0, which made a wipe run forever without writing anything (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/17)
* Wait at the end of a wipe until all data is on the device, and fail if it couldn't be written (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/18)
* On Linux, --verify reads the data from the device itself and not from the cache of the system (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/19)
* On Linux, write to the device itself and not into the cache of the system, which is faster, shows the real progress and reports a failed write at once (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/20)
* Fail if a device takes no more data before its end is reached (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/20)
* On macOS, still wait until the system wrote all data if the drive can't be told to write out its cache (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/24)
* Write blocks to the device from the memory they were generated in instead of copying them first, which makes wipes of very fast SSDs faster. For this, --blocksize is rounded up to a multiple of 4096 bytes (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/25)
* --verify reads the device in a thread of its own and compares the data without copying it first, which makes it more than twice as fast on fast SSDs (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/26)
* --verify shows how much data differs and where that begins as soon as it finds it, and not only at the end (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/27)
* Reuse the memory of the blocks instead of taking new memory for each block, and on Linux ask for huge pages for the blocks and for the data that is read. This makes wipes and --verify of very fast SSDs faster, because reading and writing takes the system a fraction of the time (https://github.com/smessmer/hddwiper/pull/28)

2.0.0
----------
Reimplement in Rust

1.x
